 {
  "id": "L22/p1",
  "lesson": "L22",
  "kind": "prediction",
  "prompt": "<p>How many tokens per second can one sequence generate?</p>",
  "answer": "b",
  "answerKind": "choice",
  "tol": 0,
  "options": [
   {
    "key": "a",
    "html": "<p>About 20,000: 300 trillion divided by 14 billion.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "b",
    "html": "<p>About 150: every token reads all 13.5 GB of weights from memory, at about 2 TB/s.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "c",
    "html": "<p>About 1, because attention is quadratic.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "d",
    "html": "<p>It depends only on the length of the prompt.</p>"
   }
  ],
  "explain": "<p>Generating a token touches every weight once but does only two operations per weight: an arithmetic intensity of about 1 FLOP per byte, far below the A100's ridge point of about 150 (L11). The bandwidth sets the speed, about 150 tokens per second, using under 1% of the arithmetic. Serving many sequences at once reuses each weight read for every sequence in the batch, which is where all of inference's tricks begin.</p>"
 },
 {
